.TH MAN 1 "2026" "spicule" "spicule User Commands"
.SH NAME
man \- find and display a manual page
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B man
.RB [ \-k ]
.I name
.RI [ name ]\ .\|.\|.
.br
.B man
.RI [ section ]
.I name
.RI [ name ]\ .\|.\|.
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B man
finds and formats the manual page for each
.I name
operand and writes it to standard output, or through a pager (see
.B PAGER
below) when standard output is a terminal.
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
.B \-k
Interpret every
.I name
operand as a keyword instead of a page name: search each page's NAME
line under
.B $MANPATH
and print every match as
.IR name (section)
\- description. Mutually exclusive with a leading
.I section
operand.
.TP
.B \-\-
Marks the end of options; every remaining argument is treated as a
.I section
or
.I name
operand, even one that begins with
.BR \- .
.SH OPERANDS
.TP
.I section
A manual section, restricting the search to that section only (e.g.
.B man 3 printf
looks only in section 3). A common, near-universal extension; not
part of the base POSIX synopsis, which is only
.BR "man [\-k] name..." .
Recognized only when at least one
.I name
operand follows it and
.B \-k
was not given.
.TP
.I name
A keyword (with
.BR \-k )
or manual page name.
.SH ENVIRONMENT
.TP
.B MANPATH
Colon-separated list of directories to search, each containing
.BR man1 / ,
.BR man2 / ,
and so on. If unset or empty, the fallback is
.BR /usr/share/man:/usr/local/share/man .
.TP
.B MANSECT
Colon-separated default section search order, used when no
.I section
operand is given. Default:
.BR 1:2:3:4:5:6:7:8:9 .
.TP
.B PAGER
The pager program to run, with the formatted page as its last
argument, when standard output is a terminal. Split on whitespace only
\(em no shell quoting is understood, so a
.B PAGER
value containing a quoted or escaped space will not work as expected.
If unset, or if it fails to run, a minimal built-in
.B \-\-More\-\-
pager is used instead, prompting once per screenful.
.TP
.B COLUMNS
Output width to wrap text to, used only when the terminal size cannot
otherwise be determined. Ignored unless it parses as a positive
integer.
.TP
.B LINES
Number of screen rows per page for the built-in pager, used only when
the terminal size cannot otherwise be determined. Ignored unless it
parses as a positive integer.
.SH APPLICATION USAGE
When standard output is not a terminal (redirected to a file, or
piped), the formatted page is written straight through with no
paging, and bold and underlined text is rendered using classic
backspace-overstrike sequences rather than color, matching the
traditional
.B \-Tascii
convention \(em pipe the output through
.BR col (1)
with its
.B \-b
option to strip the overstrike sequences back to plain text.
.PP
A page found only in gzip-compressed form is reported by name rather
than silently treated as missing; compressed pages are not
decompressed or displayed.
.PP
Table
.RB ( .TS / .TE )
and equation
.RB ( .EQ / .EN )
markup is not rendered: the table or equation's own content is
omitted, but the rest of the page still renders normally.
.SH EXIT STATUS
.TP
.B 0
Every requested page was found and displayed (or, with
.BR \-k ,
at least one keyword match was found).
.TP
.B >0
No manual entry was found for one or more
.I name
operands (or, with
.BR \-k ,
no keyword matched anything).
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR true (1),
.BR sh (1)
